I'm entirely lacking context here, so it's hard to say anything specific.
But: one design goal of Stacked Borrows (and for now I intend to maintain that for as we keep evolving our aliasing model) is that lifetimes don't matter. If the *only* difference between two programs, at the MIR level, is their lifetimes, then they will behave the same also in terms of UB. Stacked Borrows care about when a reference is used (where "use" includes all forms of dereferencing, in particular reborrowing), but it doesn't care if the lifetime goes on way after the last use of a reference.

In other words, this function is totally fine for Stacked Borrows:
```rust
pub fn write(x: &mut i32, val: i32) {
    let static_x: &'static mut i32 = unsafe { std::mem::transmute(x) };
    *static_x = val;
}
```

Of course, differences in lifetimes could lead to rustc picking different trait implementations or some such thing, and that *will* affect behavior.

