This is a slightly different behavior than what `tracing`'s `Span` type does, and (potentially) subtly incorrect if multiple subscribers are in use. 

The `Span` struct [tracks the dispatcher that observed the span's creation](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tracing/blob/0da8e3f37636845ece6bdceeecb8a0946cc77076/tracing/src/span.rs#L363-L367) (the call to `new_span`) and gave it an `Id`. Subsequently, when the span is entered, exited, or dropped, it [ensures that those methods are called on the original subscriber](https://github.com/tokio-rs/tracing/blob/0da8e3f37636845ece6bdceeecb8a0946cc77076/tracing/src/span.rs#L716-L718), even if it has moved to the context of a _different_ subscriber. 

This is because span ID assignment is only guaranteed to be unique on a per-subscriber basis. A different subscriber may have assigned the same ID to an unrelated span. If multiple subscribers are in use, this could result in a messed-up trace. 

I think we could implement the same behavior as `tracing::Span` here if we used [`Span::with_subscriber`](https://docs.rs/tracing/0.1.18/tracing/span/struct.Span.html#method.with_subscriber), like this:
```suggestion
        span.with_subscriber(|(id, dispatch)| {
            dispatch.enter(id);
        });
```

